      count: 1-4 per object (determines output color)
      shape: small filled rectangle, one of 1x1, 1x2, 2x1, 2x2
      placement: strictly interior (>=1 cell from bbox edge)
      separation: Manhattan distance >=2 between any two holes (never 4-adjacent)
  layout:
    non-overlapping bboxes
    min Manhattan distance between objects >= 2 (1-cell bg gap minimum)
    objects may touch grid border [observed]
  output:
    same shape as input; bg preserved; each object recolored by hole count
    palette: 1->BLUE, 2->RED, 3->GREEN, 4->ORANGE
    hole positions remain bg

generation_strategy:
  1. Choose grid size 16-19 x 16-18; pick one fg color (e.g. GRAY)
  2. Choose 4-6 object specs, each with a random hole count 1-4
  3. For each object, pick bbox dimensions (h 3-9, w 3-7) large enough to fit its holes
  4. Fill bbox with fg, then carve holes (each 1x1/1x2/2x1/2x2) at random interior positions
     ensuring each hole >=1 from border and Manhattan >=2 from other holes
  5. Verify hole_count matches target via the solver's 4-connected enclosed-region check
  6. Place objects on grid with >=2 Manhattan-distance separation using greedy or rejection
  7. Output = copy input, recolor each object's fg pixels by the hole-count palette
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

